1
00:00:02,355 --> 00:00:04,646
So let's start with CRUD. What does CRUD

2
00:00:04,774 --> 00:00:07,274
stand for and what is it all about?

3
00:00:08,347 --> 00:00:11,408
The C in CRUD stands for create,

4
00:00:12,193 --> 00:00:15,221
and it basically describes the process of

5
00:00:15,670 --> 00:00:18,474
storing data somewhere on the server. Or if

6
00:00:18,538 --> 00:00:21,390
we have a look at it in the bigger picture,

7
00:00:21,438 --> 00:00:23,922
so to say, sending data from a client to a

8
00:00:23,922 --> 00:00:26,133
server to then store it on the server. That

9
00:00:26,133 --> 00:00:27,847
is what create is all about.

10
00:00:28,777 --> 00:00:31,518
Read is about reading data. So here we're

11
00:00:31,518 --> 00:00:33,618
talking about retrieving data from the

12
00:00:33,618 --> 00:00:36,135
server and sending it back to the client so

13
00:00:36,167 --> 00:00:37,450
that it can be shown there.

14
00:00:38,075 --> 00:00:38,844
And as you can tell,

15
00:00:39,373 --> 00:00:41,153
create and read are 2

16
00:00:41,858 --> 00:00:43,926
things we are already doing in the

17
00:00:43,942 --> 00:00:45,609
application we built thus far.

18
00:00:46,267 --> 00:00:48,463
There, if you take a closer look in our

19
00:00:48,671 --> 00:00:50,787
goals.ts file in the routes folder,

20
00:00:51,316 --> 00:00:54,667
we have the addGoal POST route here where we

21
00:00:54,683 --> 00:00:55,757
essentially store,

22
00:00:56,366 --> 00:00:59,493
where we create a new goal on the server.

23
00:01:00,070 --> 00:01:02,748
Of course, only here in our temporary dummy

24
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:05,265
array, which is only stored in memory, but

25
00:01:05,265 --> 00:01:07,429
nonetheless, we are storing data.

26
00:01:08,215 --> 00:01:10,267
And we have this GET route where we then

27
00:01:10,476 --> 00:01:13,298
retrieve the data from courseGoals and send

28
00:01:13,298 --> 00:01:15,446
it back to the client as part of the

29
00:01:15,510 --> 00:01:17,178
rendered HTML page.

30
00:01:18,316 --> 00:01:20,721
And therefore CRUD ultimately is just about

31
00:01:20,721 --> 00:01:24,362
a couple of standard procedures, standard

32
00:01:24,410 --> 00:01:26,912
features which you need in a lot of apps.

33
00:01:27,233 --> 00:01:29,768
Because it turns out that in a lot of

34
00:01:30,024 --> 00:01:30,891
applications,

35
00:01:31,548 --> 00:01:34,532
you are dealing with data being created by

36
00:01:34,532 --> 00:01:37,821
the user, data being shown and returned to

37
00:01:37,821 --> 00:01:40,195
the user, and also, and that's where the U

38
00:01:40,307 --> 00:01:43,836
comes in, data being updated potentially. A

39
00:01:43,997 --> 00:01:47,574
lot of web applications also need to support

40
00:01:47,847 --> 00:01:51,134
the updating of data. So that the client

41
00:01:51,134 --> 00:01:53,732
sends updated data to the server and there

42
00:01:53,844 --> 00:01:55,977
you wanna store it. You wanna update the

43
00:01:56,009 --> 00:01:58,558
existing data on the server with the new

44
00:01:58,671 --> 00:02:00,531
data that's coming in from the client.

45
00:02:01,236 --> 00:02:02,888
And therefore, you can probably already

46
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:04,475
guess what the D stands for.

47
00:02:05,084 --> 00:02:07,521
It stands for delete and it's about removing

48
00:02:07,570 --> 00:02:10,135
data from the server if the client wants

49
00:02:10,135 --> 00:02:10,392
that.

50
00:02:11,883 --> 00:02:14,416
And in the application we built thus far in

51
00:02:14,544 --> 00:02:15,330
this demo,

52
00:02:15,971 --> 00:02:17,543
we support creating,

53
00:02:18,554 --> 00:02:18,827
Here,

54
00:02:19,437 --> 00:02:20,160
create,

55
00:02:21,027 --> 00:02:22,086
and we support

56
00:02:22,889 --> 00:02:23,339
reading,

57
00:02:24,093 --> 00:02:26,390
but we don't support updating and deleting

58
00:02:26,422 --> 00:02:29,280
yet. And before we add that, let me quickly

59
00:02:29,296 --> 00:02:32,347
come back to the HTTP methods, the HTTP

60
00:02:32,443 --> 00:02:33,615
verbs which we're using

61
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:36,569
thus far in our application. We're using 2

62
00:02:36,682 --> 00:02:38,111
kinds of HTTP methods,

63
00:02:38,689 --> 00:02:41,162
and that's POST, which we typically use for

64
00:02:41,258 --> 00:02:42,819
creating, and GET. So

65
00:02:43,410 --> 00:02:46,201
So that means that a request sent by the

66
00:02:46,250 --> 00:02:48,865
client that wants to add data typically

67
00:02:48,897 --> 00:02:50,261
should be a POST request.

68
00:02:50,903 --> 00:02:52,876
And that's not just some theoretical

69
00:02:52,909 --> 00:02:55,684
definition. Instead, the reason for that is

70
00:02:55,797 --> 00:02:59,921
that POST requests, unlike GET requests, can

71
00:03:00,113 --> 00:03:01,092
carry data.

72
00:03:02,135 --> 00:03:04,943
GET requests got no request body.

73
00:03:05,488 --> 00:03:08,521
All the data on GET requests has to be part

74
00:03:08,617 --> 00:03:11,297
of the URL to which a GET request is sent.

75
00:03:11,827 --> 00:03:13,931
And that's where a POST request has more

76
00:03:13,931 --> 00:03:14,863
flexibility.

77
00:03:15,537 --> 00:03:18,091
That's why typically we use POST requests

78
00:03:18,091 --> 00:03:20,677
for creating data so that the data that

79
00:03:20,677 --> 00:03:23,006
should be created on the server can be

80
00:03:23,070 --> 00:03:25,014
passed from client to server.

81
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:27,953
And for read requests, we use GET because

82
00:03:27,986 --> 00:03:31,182
all we need to do is get data. We don't need

83
00:03:31,182 --> 00:03:34,025
to add any data to the request. Hence GET is

84
00:03:34,234 --> 00:03:34,908
all we need here.

85
00:03:36,322 --> 00:03:38,747
Now for updating, we therefore also

86
00:03:38,828 --> 00:03:40,881
typically use POST requests. POST, because

87
00:03:40,913 --> 00:03:43,204
we needed a request that can carry the

88
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:44,037
update data.

89
00:03:44,742 --> 00:03:46,680
Though it is worth mentioning that there

90
00:03:46,905 --> 00:03:50,061
actually also are PUT and PATCH HTTP

91
00:03:50,061 --> 00:03:53,008
methods. That's also part of the HTTP

92
00:03:53,072 --> 00:03:55,732
protocol in the end. You could use PUT and

93
00:03:55,732 --> 00:03:56,020
PATCH,

94
00:03:56,597 --> 00:03:59,801
and in theory those HTTP methods would be

95
00:03:59,801 --> 00:04:02,380
more appropriate for updating. Because like

96
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:04,863
POST, they both can carry data.

97
00:04:05,584 --> 00:04:07,827
But they also are specifically meant for

98
00:04:07,827 --> 00:04:10,358
updating resources. The only downside is

99
00:04:10,471 --> 00:04:14,317
that Browsers like that can't send PUT or

100
00:04:14,317 --> 00:04:17,604
PATCH requests. A browser by default, if

101
00:04:17,604 --> 00:04:19,720
you're not using JavaScript, that's a

102
00:04:19,768 --> 00:04:21,579
difference then, but if you're not using

103
00:04:21,675 --> 00:04:24,833
JavaScript, a browser is only able to send

104
00:04:24,897 --> 00:04:27,767
POST and GET requests. That's why since we

105
00:04:27,767 --> 00:04:29,995
have to pick either POST or GET, we're going

106
00:04:30,027 --> 00:04:32,448
with POST for updating because that allows

107
00:04:32,448 --> 00:04:33,458
us to attach data.

108
00:04:34,195 --> 00:04:36,439
And the same for deleting. There we also

109
00:04:36,519 --> 00:04:38,812
typically use POST because we can then

110
00:04:39,389 --> 00:04:41,890
attach the information for which element

111
00:04:41,890 --> 00:04:43,815
should be deleted to the request.

112
00:04:44,682 --> 00:04:46,721
In theory, there would be a DELETE HTTP

113
00:04:46,721 --> 00:04:49,146
method, but again, the browser can't use

114
00:04:49,194 --> 00:04:50,125
this like this.

115
00:04:50,783 --> 00:04:53,401
Later when we work on REST APIs, I will come

116
00:04:53,529 --> 00:04:55,938
back to PUT, PATCH, and DELETE, but for the

117
00:04:55,938 --> 00:04:58,121
moment it's all about POST and GET.

118
00:04:58,812 --> 00:05:00,851
And this is what CRUD is all about. Then

119
00:05:01,317 --> 00:05:03,404
we'll have a look at MVC later. For the

120
00:05:03,404 --> 00:05:06,118
moment, let's dig a bit deeper into CRUD and

121
00:05:06,198 --> 00:05:08,831
let's see how we can migrate our project to

122
00:05:09,008 --> 00:05:11,143
also support the U and D.
